// Secure: {{ var_name }} must be a genuine, same-origin, site-relative
// path before it is ever used as a redirect target -- mirroring
// php_laravel's own RedirectTargetAllowlistTransform (CC-LAB-0210) at the
// level this stack's sink-selecting convention (Convention 2) naturally
// supports: a sink-level guard, not a value-rewrite transform. The
// allowlist is an explicit character-class match on the WHOLE value (not
// a "does it start with a slash?" prefix check, PA-0026), requiring
// exactly one leading '/' immediately followed by an alphanumeric
// character -- which is what rejects every open-redirect bypass shape
// this op is meant to close in one check: a protocol-relative target
// ("//evil.example" -- second character is '/'), a backslash-prefixed
// target ("/\evil.example" -- second character is '\', which some
// browsers normalize to a leading slash), a triple-slash target
// ("///evil.example"), and any value that does not begin with exactly
// one '/' at all ("evil.example", "\evil.example",
// "https://evil.example", "javascript:alert(1)" -- none of these start
// with '/' immediately followed by an alphanumeric character, so none of
// them can match). Everything after that second character is limited to
// an explicit safe path/query character class (no colon, no backslash, no
// whitespace/control characters), so a scheme or a host cannot be
// smuggled in later in the string either. Anything that fails this check
// is rejected outright (HTTP 400, fail closed) rather than silently
// collapsed to a default -- Go's ops have no shared downstream sink to
// fall back to here, unlike php_laravel's transform-then-fixed-sink
// convention.
var allowedRedirectTarget = regexp.MustCompile(`^/[A-Za-z0-9][A-Za-z0-9\-_.\/?=&%]*$`)
if !allowedRedirectTarget.MatchString({{ var_name }}) {
	http.Error(w, "invalid redirect target", http.StatusBadRequest)
	return
}
w.Header().Set("Location", {{ var_name }})
w.WriteHeader(http.StatusFound)
